                <pb facs="0659" n="539" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg539"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">573 A 1/4: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-05-07T17:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 7, 1921—5
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 8—6:20 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>119. Reference Department’s 35 March 9th,<anchor n="70" xml:id="d286-f1"/> and
                    80, April 29th,<anchor n="71" xml:id="d286-f2"/> and Embassy’s 115, May
                        4th,<anchor n="71" xml:id="d286-f3"/> concerning agreement of Preliminary
                    Communications Conference. Foreign Office now informs Embassy in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi> that before expressing an opinion in
                    the matter the competent Royal ministries to which the question was referred
                    desire to be informed as to what telegraph cables America and England wish to
                    have established in contact with the Italian coast. They also desire to be
                    informed whether the conference contemplates relay stations with automatic or
                    manual transmission or stations destined for the acceptance of local telegrams.
                    Foreign Office promises immediately to examine into the matter further as soon
                    as this information is furnished.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
